Slide 1: Title
“ Hello, we’re Team 17, and our project is Transportation and Road Conditions in Cuba, New Mexico. I’m Julian Caballero, the team’s Prime Minister. With me are Holden Justus, Mariano Montenegro, and Javas Maria. 
Slide 2: Topics of Discussion 
“We used peace engineering to brainstorm ways to make transportation easier for the residents of Cuba, NM. Here are our topics of discussion; we split the project into four areas. Holden looked at travel modes, meaning the kinds of vehicles and transportation people depend on. I covered commutes and the condition of the roads in and around Cuba and travel distances. Javas looked at gasoline: how much the community uses and what it costs them. Mariano focused on inefficiencies, like breakdowns, long waits, and unreliable transit.”
Slide 3: A small village at a big crossroads
“First, some context. Cuba is a small village in Sandoval County. The Census estimate is about 462 residents, with a margin of error of plus or minus 220. About 288 of them are employed, give or take 97.
But that number is misleading, because Cuba is a service hub for thousands of rural residents around it. That includes Navajo Nation chapter communities to the west. The Cuba ZIP codes cover over 1,800 square miles. And the nearest big city, Albuquerque, is about 83 miles away. So distance is a big part of this story.”
Slide 4: The road network
“Here’s how the roads are laid out. The main route is US 550, a four-lane divided highway. It’s the lifeline of the area, connecting Bernalillo and Albuquerque to the south with Farmington to the north. In town, the clinic, the post office and the stores all line that one highway.
Off of 550, state roads branch out: NM 126 toward the Jemez Mountains, NM 197 toward Torreon and the Navajo chapters, and NM 96 toward Abiquiu.
Past those, most rural homes are reached by unpaved county and BIA roads, gravel or dirt. These are the roads that rut, wash out and turn to mud. That’s where a lot of the problems we’ll show you begin.”
Slide 5: Commutes compared with the US
“That brings us to commutes. About 53 percent of workers in the wider Cuba area travel short distances to work. You might think that’s a good thing, but it means people are on these local roads every day, and those are the roads in the worst shape. In a lot of the country, commuters spend most of their trip on maintained highways. In Cuba, the daily trip often depends on gravel and dirt.
